==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|overtaken}}, likely a replacement alteration (as the Middle English verb {{m|enm|taken}} replaced {{m|enm|nimen|t=to take}}), of {{der|en|enm|overnimen|t=to overtake}}, from {{der|en|ang|oferniman|t=to take by surprise, overtake}}, equivalent to {{pre|en|over|take}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|en|verb}}
** {{IPA|en|/əʊ̯vəːˈteɪ̯k/|a=RP}}
*** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-overtake.wav|a=Southern England}}
** {{IPA|en|/oʊ̯vɚˈteɪ̯k/|a=GA,CA}}
*** {{audio|en|en-us-overtake.ogg|a=US}}
* {{a|en|noun}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈəʊ̯vəːteɪ̯k/|a=RP}}
*** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-overtake2.wav|a=Southern England}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈoʊ̯vɚteɪ̯k/|a=GA,CA}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪk|s=3}}
* {{hyph|en|o|ver|take}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|~s|overtaking|overtook|~n}}

# To [[pass]] a slower moving [[object]] or [[entity]] (on the side closest to oncoming traffic).
#: {{syn|en|forge}}
#: {{ant|en|[[undertake]]<t:to pass a slower moving vehicle on the [[curbside]]>}}
#: {{ux|en|The racehorse '''overtook''' the lead pack on the last turn.}}
#: {{ux|en|The car was so slow we were '''overtaken''' by a bus.}}
#* {{RQ:Collins No Name|volume=II|chapter=II|pages=176–177|pageref=176|passage="I won't over-walk myself," he said, cheerfully. "If the coach doesn't '''overtake''' me on the road, I can wait for it where I stop to breakfast. Dry your eyes, my dear; and give me a kiss."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To become [[greater]] than something else in quantity, worth, etc.
#: {{ux|en|Grocery sales in the north have '''overtaken''' those in the south.}}
# To take by [[surprise]]; surprise and [[overcome]]; [[carry away]].
#: {{ux|en|Our plans were '''overtaken''' by events.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Sonnets|sonnet=34|page=51|passage=VVhy didſt thou promiſe ſuch a beautious day, / And make me trauaile forth vvithout my cloake, / To let [[base#Adjective|bace]] cloudes '''ore-take''' me in my vvay, / Hiding thy [[bravery|brau'ry]] in their rotten ſmoke.}}

